{
  "raw": {
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=5002f815-f2bc-425b-8aeb-2c6546282bcd": {
      "title": "Director of Financial Services",
      "description": "Director of Financial Services. Franklin University, a private, independent, accredited, nonprofit university located in Columbus, Ohio, invites applications for the position of Director, Financial Services. Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults balancing educational goals with work, family, and other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin is recognized as an innovator in online education and serves students through applied undergraduate, master's, and doctoral programs designed to support career advancement and lifelong learning. More than 50,000 Franklin alumni live and work around the world, making meaningful contributions to their professions and communities. Position Summary The Director, Financial Services provides operational oversight for the University’s budgeting, forecasting, financial reporting, financial analysis, and financial planning processes and systems. This position coordinates the annual budget process, ongoing forecasting and management reporting, supervises the Financial Analyst and Collections Analyst, and develops financial analysis to support University decision-making. The role serves as a key resource for evaluating financial performance, identifying trends, risks, and opportunities, and translating financial and operational data into clear information for University leadership. Responsibilities Budget Administration and Forecasting Analyze key budget and forecast drivers, including enrollment-based tuition revenue, discounts, salaries, benefits, and operating expenses, and identify significant trends, risks, and opportunities. Coordinate and administer the annual operating budget process, including budget timelines, departmental submissions, review and validation of budget inputs, model updates, and budget communications. Review departmental budget submissions for completeness, accuracy, and alignment with University priorities. Develop and review budget variance analyses, financial projections, and management reporting to evaluate performance and support decision-making. Prepare and maintain forecast models by reviewing actual results, evaluating changes in assumptions, and incorporating known risks and opportunities into projected financial performance. Support multi-year financial planning, scenario modeling, and related financial analysis Financial Reporting, Analytics, and System Administration Develop and maintain financial reports, dashboards, analytical datasets, and management reporting tools using data from multiple University systems. Administer the University’s budgeting, forecasting, and financial reporting platform, including cost center structures, reporting hierarchies, system configuration, data integrity, and recurring reporting processes. Develop or review financial models and projections for new program proposals, University initiatives, and operational reviews. Oversee completion and review of recurring financial survey, accreditation, regulatory, and industry reporting requests prepared by the Financial Analyst. Recommend improvements to reporting tools, budget processes, system workflows, and recurring Financial Services procedures. Staff Leadership and Operational Oversight Supervise the Financial Analyst and Collections Analyst, including setting priorities, managing workloads, reviewing key work products, and supporting professional development. Oversee financial reporting activities performed by staff and review key reports for accuracy and completeness. Ensure key Financial Services processes are documented and cross-trained to maintain continuity and support effective team operations. Financial Services Administration Provide oversight and coordination for recurring Financial Services processes, including investment administration and reporting support, insurance renewal coordination, collections reporting, and related operational activities. Support financial analysis for strategic projects, new initiatives, and ongoing operations as assigned. Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in Finance, Accounting, Business Administration, Economics, or related field. Five or more years of progressively responsible experience in budgeting, forecasting, financial reporting, financial planning, or financial analysis. Advanced analytical and financial modeling skills, including working with large datasets, using SQL or other query tools to extract and analyze data, identifying trends, key drivers, and emerging risks Experience leading or coordinating budget development, forecasting, financial reporting, and variance analysis activities. Experience working with budgeting, planning, reporting, or business intelligence systems. Supervisory, team leadership, or project leadership experience. Ability to manage multiple priorities, meet deadlines, and communicate financial information clearly to financial and non-financial audiences. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in Business, Finance, Accounting, or a related field. Higher education finance experience. Experience administering budgeting, forecasting, planning, or reporting software. Experience supporting or coordinating investment reporting, insurance administration, collections operations, regulatory reporting, or related Financial Services functions. Additional Information This position follows an on-site hybrid work schedule and requires flexibility to meet departmental and university needs. Occasional evening and weekend availability may be required to address sensitive or time-sensitive student matters, and some travel may be expected. Franklin offers a competitive benefits package, incentive program, and free parking. In addition, Franklin provides free undergraduate tuition for employees and eligible family members, graduate tuition assistance for full-time employees and eligible family members, and a discount on doctoral tuition for employees. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=7e9b2698-16e5-460d-bce0-0e1d88f413f8": {
      "title": "Director, Student Wellbeing and Access",
      "description": "Director, Student Wellbeing and Access. Franklin University, a private, independent, accredited, nonprofit university located in Columbus, Ohio, invites applications for the position of Director, Student Wellbeing & Access. Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults balancing educational goals with work, family, and other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin is recognized as an innovator in online education and serves students through applied undergraduate, master's, and doctoral programs designed to support career advancement and lifelong learning. More than 50,000 Franklin alumni live and work around the world, making meaningful contributions to their professions and communities. Position Summary The Director, Student Wellbeing & Access will provide strategic and operational leadership for initiatives that promote student wellbeing, accessibility, accountability, persistence, and success at Franklin University. Reporting to the Dean of Students, this role oversees Accessibility Services, Student Wellbeing, and Community Standards, while serving as the University's ADA/Section 504 Coordinator and Title IX Coordinator. The Director will lead efforts to identify and address barriers that may interfere with student engagement and academic progress, developing innovative, scalable approaches to student support that meet the needs of Franklin's diverse and geographically dispersed student population. This includes advancing disability access and accommodations, strengthening student wellbeing resources, and providing oversight for complex student matters and institutional compliance responsibilities. As a key member of the Dean of Students leadership team, the Director will foster collaboration across the University, leverage data and technology to improve student services, and promote a culture of accountability, compassion, continuous improvement, and student-centered support. Key Responsibilities The Director provides strategic and operational leadership for Accessibility Services, Student Wellbeing, and Community Standards, ensuring these functions support student engagement, persistence, and success. The Director serves as the University's ADA/Section 504 Coordinator and Title IX Coordinator, overseeing disability accommodations, institutional accessibility, regulatory compliance, and related policies and procedures. This role leads the development of proactive, scalable, and virtual-first student wellbeing initiatives designed to address nonacademic barriers to student success, including oversight of the Student Emergency Fund and partnerships that expand access to student resources and support. The Director also provides leadership for student conduct, grievances, appeals, and other complex student matters, ensuring fair, consistent, and timely resolution. Responsibilities include coordinating responses to concerns submitted through the University's anonymous reporting system and participating on the Franklin Intervention & Awareness Team (FIAT). The Director supervises and develops professional staff, collaborates with University leaders to strengthen student support and institutional practices, and leverages data, technology, and process improvements to enhance service delivery and operational effectiveness. As a member of the Dean of Students leadership team, the Director promotes a culture of accountability, compassion, innovation, and continuous improvement while exercising sound judgment and discretion in sensitive matters. Minimum Qualifications A master's degree in higher education, student affairs, counseling, rehabilitation counseling, education, law, public administration, social work, or a related field is required, along with a minimum of five years of progressively responsible experience in higher education. Candidates must have meaningful experience in postsecondary disability or accessibility services, including demonstrated knowledge of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), Section 504, the interactive accommodation process, and related regulatory requirements. The successful candidate will have supervisory experience, a broad understanding of Student Affairs practices, and experience supporting students with complex needs or circumstances affecting their success. Demonstrated ability to interpret and apply institutional policies and regulatory requirements, including student conduct, grievances, investigations, or related resolution processes, is essential. Knowledge of Title IX requirements, strong leadership and communication skills, sound judgment, and the ability to manage sensitive and confidential matters with discretion, neutrality, and compassion are also required. Additional Information This position follows an on-site hybrid work schedule and requires flexibility to meet departmental and university needs. Occasional evening and weekend availability may be required to address sensitive or time-sensitive student matters, and some travel may be expected. Franklin offers a competitive benefits package, incentive program, and free parking. In addition, Franklin provides free undergraduate tuition for employees and eligible family members, graduate tuition assistance for full-time employees and eligible family members, and a discount on doctoral tuition for employees. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=4e222519-d1ac-42d7-8e2d-4ceb3a4e0dd7": {
      "title": "Center for Teaching Excellence Student Assistant",
      "description": "Center for Teaching Excellence Student Assistant. Position Summary: The Center for Teaching Excellence Student Assistant will work closely with the Coordinator, CTE to provide general support by assisting with clerical duties, special projects, customer support and tracking, editing and preparing documents, filling and scanning documents, assisting with routine processes and other miscellaneous duties as assigned. Duties and Responsibilities: Provide general clerical support to staff in the department Schedule meetings & rooms Prepare meeting documents Deliver/pickup documents across campus Mail documents & packages Data entry Assist with customer support and tracking Respond to routine customer inquiries via email & phone Track all CTE inquiries Assist with tracking and reporting course registration Monitor event registration system Facilitate processes for new instructors Scan & file documents Assist with digital archiving and management of CTE documents Assist with basic CTE processes Generate Purchase Orders, Purchase Requisitions, & Faculty Stipend forms Assist with the contracting & tracking processes Request certificates Update training spreadsheets Minimum Requirements/Qualifications: • Must be a current Franklin University student • Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications • Excellent organizational and communication skills • Comfortable with repetitive processes • Strong customer services skills • Availability to work up to 19 hours per week, Monday – Friday between 8 am and 5 pm Preferred Skills and Characteristics: Experience with AI and Chatbot development Additional Notes: Schedule may be flexible and vary based on the availability of the candidate and the needs of the department. Position will be hybrid – at least half time in-person. About Franklin University Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults who must balance their educational goals with other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin has earned its long-standing reputation as an innovator in online education. The University offers applied, in-demand undergraduate, master’s and doctorate programs that enable adult learners to achieve their educational and professional goals. From entrepreneurs to CEOs, more than 50,000 Franklin alumni can be found around the world contributing to the communities in which they live and work. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=6df93c62-f6ac-4f60-b7c8-1099598d882a": {
      "title": "Learning Commons Student Assistant",
      "description": "Learning Commons Student Assistant. Franklin University is seeking a part-time student employee to work up to 19 hours per week in the Learning Commons. The position provides in-person support to students, staff, and faculty during operating hours, including evenings until 6 p.m. Applicants must be enrolled in Franklin University courses to be eligible. Student Assistants complete daily opening and closing procedures for the Learning Commons; help customers locate items and process resource requests; collect and report statistics; answer phone inquiries; and troubleshoot printer and copier issues. They also support the department’s digitization efforts and assist staff with other projects as needed. Qualifications: Reliable transportation and consistent punctuality. Strong customer service skills. Creative thinking and proven problem-solving skills. Clear verbal and written communication skills. Skill in prioritizing multiple tasks and collaborating effectively within a team. Intermediate proficiency in Microsoft Word and Excel. Capacity to learn new technologies, including the library’s catalog system and databases. The candidate must possess a strong customer-centered philosophy and understand and agree with Franklin's relationship management principles and University values. Franklin offers a positive work environment and free parking for Franklin employees. About Franklin University Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults who must balance their educational goals with other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin has earned its long-standing reputation as an innovator in online education. The University offers applied, in-demand undergraduate, master’s and doctorate programs that enable adult learners to achieve their educational and professional goals. From entrepreneurs to CEOs, more than 50,000 Franklin alumni can be found around the world contributing to the communities in which they live and work. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=a3db66e6-e2e3-42c2-97fb-50f840ba3d0c": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, Radiologic Technology",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty, Radiologic Technology. Position Summary: Franklin University, a private, independent, accredited, not-for-profit University located in Columbus, Ohio invites applicants for its Adjunct Faculty-Radiologic Technology position. The Adjunct Faculty role includes primary responsibility for providing quality instruction and maintaining a positive learning environment with an emphasis placed on teaching, supporting and evaluating students. Assigned courses will be for online instruction. Courses Available to Teach and Course Outcomes: HIM 150: Medical Terminology Identify common terminology, tools, and practices used in health and wellness domains. Interpret medical terms using prefixes, word roots, suffixes, and combining forms. Apply medical terminology conventions to construct and define healthcare terms. Recognize terminology associated with body systems, diseases, diagnostic procedures, and treatments. Use medical terminology appropriately within healthcare and clinical settings. RADT 101: Introduction to Radiologic Technology Apply patient assessment skills for radiological procedures. Implement safety protocols for radiographic procedures. Apply ethical and legal standards in patient care. Recommend appropriate responses to emergency situations, Apply humanistic and cultural humility approaches to decision-making. RADT 102: Radiation Science: Physics, Biology, and Protection Explain the fundamentals of X-ray production, Differentiate how radiation interacts with matter, Describe the characteristics of X-rays. Evaluate the biological effects of ionizing radiation on cells and tissues. Apply radiation protection strategies. Evaluate the roles of federal & state regulatory bodies in radiation safety compliance. RADT 110: Radiographic Procedures I Explain radiological positioning terminology, Apply anatomical knowledge to radiological positioning techniques, Analyze anatomy on image capture Assess radiographic images in terms of positioning accuracy, image quality, anatomical structures visualized, and appropriate exposure considerations. RADT 120: Radiographic Procedures II Explain radiological positioning terminology. Apply anatomical knowledge to radiological positioning techniques. Analyze anatomy on image capture. Assess radiographic images in terms of positioning accuracy, image quality, anatomical structures visualized, and appropriate exposure considerations. Explain the purpose, preparation, and execution of contrast materials RADT 201: Image Acquisition, Equipment, and Quality Assurance Evaluate the effects of exposure factors on image quality. Analyze factors affecting spatial resolution, contrast resolution, image noise, and distortion. Discuss the components of computed radiography (CR) and digital radiography (DR) systems. Explain Quality Assurance (QA) and Quality Control (QC) in imaging. Describe regulatory standards governing equipment performance and radiation safety. Evaluate the role of the radiology professional on data quality RADT 210: Radiographic Procedures III Explain radiological positioning terminology Apply anatomical knowledge to radiological positioning techniques Analyze anatomy on image capture Assess radiographic images in terms of positioning accuracy, image quality, anatomical structures visualized, and appropriate exposure considerations. Interpret sectional imaging of key anatomical regions, including cross-sectional relationships as visualized in CT, MRI, and other imaging modalities RADT 205: Radiologic Technology Review Utilize structured review sessions to enhance test-taking skills and confidence in preparation for national certification exams. Synthesize key concepts from radiologic technology courses to develop a thorough understanding of radiologic technology. Assess imaging parameters to meet quality assurance standards in clinical practice. Apply radiologic technology principles to minimize exposure risks for patients, personnel, and the public. Duties and Responsibilities: Teaches courses in discipline Maintains attendance records, determines and submits grades with established timeframes established by the University Creates a welcoming, inclusive and productive learning environment Supports the resolution of student issues related to course instruction Provides feedback on what is working and what is not with the course Participates in all department meetings and required training Participates in personal professional development Performs other duties as assigned by the Lead Faculty Minimum Requirements/Qualifications: Bachelor of Science degree in Radiologic Sciences, or other healthcare or science-related field Master’s Degree in Public Health, Health Informatics, Health Information Management, or other health-related field for Medical Terminology course Holds current American Registry of Radiologic Technologists (ARRT) certification and registration, or equivalent, in radiography (R) Documents two years’ clinical experience in the professional discipline Holds academic and/or professional credentials appropriate to the subject content area taught Is knowledgeable of course development, instruction, evaluation, and academic advising Commitment to collaborative & collegiate relationships Maintain confidential and sensitive information Knowledge of higher education regulations, compliance and accrediting agencies Computer proficiency Strong organizational and interpersonal skills Ability to communicate in a professional manner and work effectively with faculty, staff, students and members of the professional community Excellent multi-tasking skills Ability to work in a fast-paced environment Maintains a comprehensive knowledge of the radiologic technology discipline in order to accurately and effectively educate students while optimizing patient care/safety Instructors are required to comply with all accreditation and regulatory requirements Preferred Skills and Characteristics: Master of Science degree, Master of Healthcare Administration (MHA), or other healthcare or related field Five years of clinical experience in the field of Radiologic Technology 1-3 years teaching in a post-secondary Joint Review Committee on Education in Radiologic Technology (JRCERT) accredited Radiologic Technology program Additional certifications in other modalities, i.e., Computerized Tomography (CT), Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MR), Cardiovascular Interventional or Vascular Interventional (CI/VI), Nuclear Medicine (NM), Sonography (S), Vascular Sonography (VS), Mammography (M) We are not able to hire in California, New York, Massachusetts, Montana, or the City of Philadelphia at this time. About Franklin University Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults who must balance their educational goals with other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin has earned its long-standing reputation as an innovator in online education. The University offers applied, in-demand undergraduate, master’s and doctorate programs that enable adult learners to achieve their educational and professional goals. From entrepreneurs to CEOs, more than 50,000 Franklin alumni can be found around the world contributing to the communities in which they live and work. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status. We embrace diversity and strive to create an environment where students, faculty and staff feel a sense of belonging. We recognize that our collective potential can only be realized when difference is valued, equal access to opportunity is sought, and inclusion is practiced."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=19092e3c-38a1-48da-a836-ad8d678a13c1": {
      "title": "Accreditation Specialist",
      "description": "Accreditation Specialist. Franklin University, a private, independent, accredited, nonprofit university located in Columbus, Ohio, invites applications for the position of Accreditation Specialist. Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults balancing educational goals with work, family, and other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin is recognized as an innovator in online education and serves students through applied undergraduate, master's, and doctoral programs designed to support career advancement and lifelong learning. More than 50,000 Franklin alumni live and work around the world, making meaningful contributions to their professions and communities. Position Summary: The Accreditation Specialist ensures Franklin University’s compliance with the requirements of state higher education agencies and professional licensure boards, the Higher Learning Commission, international governing bodies, and specialized accreditors. The Accreditation Specialist employs best practices in the completion of applications and reports for federal, state, and international higher education agencies, the approval of which makes it possible for the University to offer programs in new locations and maintain accreditation/authorization of existing programs. This position maintains positive working relationships with the University’s faculty and staff, as well as external compliance representatives. Additional responsibilities include monitoring state regulations (including those specific to professional licensure boards) and specialized accreditation requirements for communication of noteworthy changes to University stakeholders and ensuring ongoing compliance for our academic offerings. In addition, the Accreditation Specialist will assist the office of Accreditation and Authorization with general projects related to obtaining and maintaining accreditation and state authorization for the University. This position reports to the Assistant Director, Accreditation and Authorization. Duties and Responsibilities: Complete applications and other documents required for authorization/licensure in U.S. states & territories, and foreign countries as applicable based on University operations and authorization/licensure regulations Employ best practices for submission of information to state, regional, specialized, and international accrediting/authorizing agencies Remain current on state and federal legislation and other regulations relating to authorization/licensure of higher education programs and professional licensure programs Monitor University activities and initiatives and take necessary action to ensure University and program compliance with institutional accreditation, specialized accreditation, and state authorization/licensure requirements Develop and maintain strong relationships with: University faculty and staff, state agencies of higher education, state professional licensure board agencies, specialized accreditors, and individuals in the state authorization community at other colleges and universities, including attending and/or supporting meetings held virtually, in-person at the University’s main campus, and/or in-person at locations outside of the Columbus, Ohio area Create and support effective document storage and retrieval procedures for files created and utilized by the office of Accreditation and Authorization Assist in project management for major institutional and specialized accreditation efforts, such as self-study reports and site visits. Responsibilities will include document creation and submission, collaboration with key stakeholders, and logistics planning Assist with other Accreditation & Authorization projects as needed, such as site visit planning and execution, and coordination of the State Authorization Network of Ohio (SAN-O) annual meeting with representatives from other Universities Minimum Requirements/Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree Minimum of three years' experience in a professional position Research skills to review state, federal, and international regulations and to identify requirements for authorization/approval Adept at using multiple software applications, including Adobe Acrobat and Microsoft Office 365 (including Teams, Word, Excel, and Teams) Excellent verbal and written communication skills Organized, detail-oriented, and proactive Must be dependable and self-motivated Ability to work collaboratively with internal and external constituents Maintain a high degree of confidentiality Preferred Skills and Characteristics: Experience in working with government regulatory and compliance agencies Experience with higher education, academics, and/or accreditation/state authorization Experience working with education programs that lead to professional licensure in the fields of education or healthcare Additional Notes: This position follows an on-site hybrid work schedule and requires flexibility to meet departmental and university needs. Franklin offers a competitive benefits package, incentive program, and free parking. In addition, Franklin provides free undergraduate tuition for employees and eligible family members, graduate tuition assistance for full-time employees and eligible family members, and a discount on doctoral tuition for employees. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=a1e95045-c47e-4647-95ca-d44938996a92": {
      "title": "Part-Time Learning Commons Mathematics and Statistics Tutor",
      "description": "Part-Time Learning Commons Mathematics and Statistics Tutor. Franklin University is seeking an enthusiastic, dependable, flexible, and self-motivated mathematics and statistics tutor to join the Learning Commons team. The successful candidate will communicate clearly, support students effectively, and demonstrate strong knowledge of the subjects they tutor. Requirements: Candidates must hold a bachelor’s degree or higher in mathematics or a related field and have prior teaching or tutoring experience. Strong communication skills and demonstrated proficiency in pre-algebra, fundamental algebra, college algebra, applied calculus, and statistics are required. Candidates must be able to provide live, online tutoring in a synchronous environment and create an engaging, supportive learning experience for students. Hours: Hours vary based on tutoring demand. Evening and weekend availability is required. Note: We are not able to hire candidates from New York, California, Massachusetts, Montana, and the City of Philadelphia."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=d041df9f-83e3-40ad-b30d-77fd3299ed0f": {
      "title": "Student Financial Services Associate",
      "description": "Student Financial Services Associate. Franklin University, a private, independent, accredited, nonprofit university located in Columbus, Ohio, invites applications for the position of Student Financial Services Associate. Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults balancing educational goals with work, family, and other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin is recognized as an innovator in online education and serves students through applied undergraduate, master's, and doctoral programs designed to support career advancement and lifelong learning. More than 50,000 Franklin alumni live and work around the world, making meaningful contributions to their professions and communities. Position Summary: Under the general direction of a Student Financial Services (SFS) Lead, the Student Financial Services Associate is responsible for providing financial aid and account counseling/advisement to students. As an initial point of contact for the Financial Aid and Business Offices, the SFS Associate’s job responsibilities include (but are not limited to) educating students on financial aid and balance status, analyzing their overall financial circumstances, and processing a variety of documentation related to professional judgment, student eligibility, the Community College Alliance program, and scholarships. The SFS Associate will also provide information regarding refunds, partnership verification and conduct outreach on needed documents and payment options. This position will also participate in and organize on-campus events that encourage student financial wellness. This position has a hybrid working schedule. Hours are Monday-Thursday:9am-6pm and Friday: 8am-5pm. Once every 3 months, an evening shift, which ends at 7 p.m., is required. Duties and Responsibilities: Perform outreach to students at financial or academic risk of losing financial aid eligibility or enrollment status and counsel them on the options and resources available. Review student accounts and advise students on all aspects of the financial aid and student account process. This includes but is not limited to payment plans, tuition deferment, billing charges, financial holds, third-party billing, scholarships, FAFSA, Entrance and Exit Counseling, and all other eligibility requirements based on individual circumstances. Process financial aid professional judgement appeals such as Satisfactory Academic Progress, Special and Unusual Circumstances. Assist students with the completion of federal FAFSA application, and other documents related to financial aid eligibility Counsel students about the Federal Direct Loan Program and assist with the online Entrance Counseling and Master Promissory Note. Counseling includes a review of NSLDS student data and the appropriate default prevention information and repayment options Review and process Default Clear letters, Pell Overpayment Clear letters, Reaffirmations, Agency Releases, and Emergency Loans Create and maintain financial aid policies and procedures for area of responsibility Participate in events to support recruitment and retention efforts, to educate students about the financial aid process, and to prepare graduating seniors for loan repayment Special projects and other duties as assigned Minimum Requirements/Qualifications: Bachelor’s Degree required At least one year of experience in student financial aid, student accounts, student affairs or related higher education fields. Excellent organizational, communication and analytical skills Demonstrated ability to successfully multi-task in a fast-paced environment and meet deadlines A desire to help students and provide excellent customer service while working closely in a team environment Proficiency with Microsoft Office Preferred Skills and Characteristics: Knowledge of accounting principles Strong knowledge of Title IV Programs and federal Financial Aid regulations Ellucian Colleague experience is a plus. Additional Information This position follows an on-site hybrid work schedule and requires flexibility to meet departmental and university needs. Franklin offers a competitive benefits package, incentive program, and free parking. In addition, Franklin provides free undergraduate tuition for employees and eligible family members, graduate tuition assistance for full-time employees and eligible family members, and a discount on doctoral tuition for employees. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=52542b40-2d81-4a85-872e-2d4f9fce565e": {
      "title": "Community Outreach Federal Work Study Assistant – Columbus Literacy Council Academic Services Tutor/Assistant",
      "description": "Community Outreach Federal Work Study Assistant – Columbus Literacy Council Academic Services Tutor/Assistant. The Academic Services Tutor/Assistant serves as an integral part of the team in assisting the student in the Tutoring Center with their academic needs which center around the CLC’s mission of increasing employability, enabling future education, encouraging civic involvement, and promoting family stability and support in our community. The Academic Services Tutor/Assistant is responsible for assisting adult student with homework, computer skills, and basic conversation, as well as helping them to achieve their literacy goals. This position will also assist with administrative tasks requiring the use of Microsoft Office and Google Application. The student should possess tutoring and problem-solving skills, demonstrate telephone and videophone etiquette, and be able to work with individuals from diverse backgrounds. Applicants must be current Franklin University students with Federal Work Study eligibility as part of their financial aid award package. Academic Services Tutor/Assistant will assist the CLC Tutoring Center located in one of the main offices located in the Jefferson Center located at 92 Jefferson Avenue, Columbus, Ohio 43215."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=da6c06b9-f11b-4334-a6c1-45e2875fd28d": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, Data Analytics (Advanced Analytics)",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty, Data Analytics (Advanced Analytics). Franklin University, a private, independent, accredited, not-for-profit University located in Columbus, Ohio invites applicants for its Adjunct Faculty, Advanced Analytics position during our Spring 2027 term. The Adjunct Faculty role includes primary responsibility for providing quality instruction and maintaining a positive learning environment with an emphasis placed on teaching, supporting and evaluating students. Position Summary: This post is for a part-time adjunct faculty position to teach a graduate-level course, Advanced Analytics, in an online format (12 weeks). This course examines the data analysis process with the emphasis of quantitative and qualitative findings from data. Students will develop skills in data analytics and predictive analytics that will allow them to develop algorithmic methods and use them along with popular industry software for data-driven solutions. The faculty should develop PowerPoint slides, lesson plans, and demonstrations and should easily be able to provide examples from a number of fields. The faculty should be knowledgeable and experienced with the statistical analysis methods and the use of various analytical tools such as R, and Python, and be able to conduct demonstrations. In addition to the educational qualifications, the candidate should possess excellent communication skills, work ethics, and passion to teach Analytics/Statistics to busy working adult students with flexibility and integrity. Duties and Responsibilities: Teaching a graduate-level analytics/statistics course according to the curriculum, content, and outcomes as specified by the Lead Faculty. The class sessions will be held online. Monitoring and advising students on course-related work, prompt correspondence via email, facilitation of course-related discussions, holding regular office hours and other assigned responsibilities corresponding to the faculty teaching assignment. Timely assessment and evaluation of student assignments including exams, homework assignments, quizzes, projects, discussions, and other activities related to the course. Participation in adjunct MATH/DATA faculty meetings/retreats and associated departmental activities as identified by the Lead Faculty for the assigned course, and responding to Lead Faculty’s requests related to course components. Minimum Requirements/Qualifications: A doctoral degree in Computer Information Systems, Information Systems, Information Technology, Computer Science, Data Science, Statistics, Mathematics or a closely related discipline is required. If the degree is in a related field, at least 18 graduate credit hours should be in the aforementioned fields. At least three years of online teaching experience in higher education. Be skilled in video conferencing platforms Be proficient in online learning management systems Have knowledge and experience with several analytics software such as Tableau, R, and Python. Preferred Skills and Characteristics: A doctoral degree in Engineering, Economics or Business with at least 18 credit hours in the field of Mathematics, Data Science, or Statistics, or doctoral degree in these fields with a graduate certificate in Data Science. At least 5 years of industry experience with specific projects that are targeting the course outcomes. Candidates with experience in data science, data analytics, data visualization, and machine/statistical learning are preferred. Any industry certifications in the field of data science or analytics About Franklin University Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults who must balance their educational goals with other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin has earned its long-standing reputation as an innovator in online education. The University offers applied, in-demand undergraduate, master’s and doctorate programs that enable adult learners to achieve their educational and professional goals. From entrepreneurs to CEOs, more than 50,000 Franklin alumni can be found around the world contributing to the communities in which they live and work. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status. We embrace diversity and strive to create an environment where students, faculty and staff feel a sense of belonging. We recognize that our collective potential can only be realized when difference is valued, equal access to opportunity is sought, and inclusion is practiced. We are not able to hire in California, New York, Massachusetts, Montana, or the City of Philadelphia."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=41ce3e68-cb04-4018-b437-4901cc98af84": {
      "title": "Student Teacher Supervisor (HR Use Only)",
      "description": "Student Teacher Supervisor (HR Use Only). Please note: This position is only intended for application by those already in discussion with Franklin University's College of Education. If you were not specifically directed to apply here by Human Resources, your application will not be reviewed . Visit our Careers page for other employment opportunities: https://www.franklin.edu/about-us/careers-at-franklin. If there is not currently an opportunity available, please take a moment to create a Presence which saves your information for a future application. You will also have the option to create Job Alerts to receive emails when new opportunities are added that meet your search criteria. Position Summary: The Student Teacher Supervisor serves as a Clinical University Supervisor for student teacher candidates, overseeing candidates in their assigned licensure area and grade band. This supervision role ensures student teacher candidates receive consistent support, guidance, and evaluation throughout their student teaching experience. Location Dependency: Your specific work mode (In-person, Remote, Hybrid) depends on your state of residence. Duties and Responsibilities: Complete all onboarding paperwork and required training sessions (including the Supervisor Training and Seminar Meet-and-Greet). Provide consistent supervision for assigned student teacher candidates through their assigned placement. Guide and support cooperating teachers through documentation and process expectations. Serve as a liaison between the H.K. and Eva James College of Education and the hosting school partner. Conduct formal student observations and post-conference meetings, plus midterm and final evaluations using the H.K. and Eva James College of Education's assessment tools. Maintain regular communication with student teachers via meetings, email, and Zoom, offering timely, constructive feedback Submit all required evaluation documents by program deadlines. Foster student confidence, teaching efficacy, and professional growth. Stay current with supervision best practices (e.g., cognitive coaching, adult learning theory, and instructional strategies). Communicate any concerns to the Clinical Field Office. The supervisor will conduct all required candidate, cooperating teacher, observation, and evaluation meetings in accordance with university and applicable state requirements. Meetings and observations may be conducted virtually, in person, or in a hybrid format as permitted by the candidate's state. An initial meeting with the student teacher candidate and cooperating teacher. Conduct the number and type of classroom observations required by the candidate's state, university program, and/or applicable educator preparation regulations, including post-observation conferences. A midterm evaluation meeting using the H.K. and Eva James College of Education's approved assessment method. Conduct the number and type of classroom observations required by the candidate's state, university program, and/or applicable educator preparation regulations, including post-observation conferences. A final evaluation meeting using the H.K. and Eva James College of Education's approved assessment method. Minimum Requirements/Qualifications: Master's degree in Education or a related field. Minimum of five years of successful K–12 teaching experience. Hold or have previously held an appropriate professional teaching license, certificate, or credential issued by a state education agency. Meet any additional supervisor qualifications required by the state in which the assigned student teacher is completing clinical practice. Demonstrated knowledge and experience within the candidate's assigned licensure/content area and grade band. Daytime availability to conduct observations, conferences, and meetings with student teacher candidates and cooperating teachers. Ability to complete supervision activities virtually, in person, or through a hybrid format as required. Familiarity with applicable state educator standards, evaluation systems, educator preparation requirements, and the InTASC Model Core Teaching Standards. Must meet applicable residency or geographic requirements associated with the assigned supervisor position. Google Workspace, Microsoft Teams, GoReact, and other online documentation/assessment platforms. Additional Notes: Supervisors receive a stipend of $750 per student teacher per semester. Assignments are matched based on licensure area and grade band. Typical supervision loads range from 1-3 students for new supervisors, with a maximum of 5 per semester. About Franklin University Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults who must balance their educational goals with other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin has earned its long-standing reputation as an innovator in online education. The University offers applied, in-demand undergraduate, master’s and doctorate programs that enable adult learners to achieve their educational and professional goals. From entrepreneurs to CEOs, more than 50,000 Franklin alumni can be found around the world contributing to the communities in which they live and work. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=0a2f15d6-9adc-4754-8df8-36a7127ae911": {
      "title": "Program Chair, Financial Planning and Financial Management",
      "description": "Program Chair, Financial Planning and Financial Management. Franklin University, located in Columbus, Ohio, is seeking a Program Chair, Financial Planning & Financial Management to lead the Bachelor of Science in Financial Planning and the Associate and Bachelor of Science in Financial Management programs within the Ross College of Business. This is a full-time, 12-month position responsible for providing strategic direction for the programs through academic leadership, curriculum oversight, faculty supervision, teaching, assessment, scholarship, and engagement with industry and professional organizations. Candidates may work in a remote capacity , with an expectation to be onsite as needed to support departmental initiatives, strategic planning, collaboration, commencement, faculty assemblies, and other University events. This is an outstanding opportunity for an experienced academic leader to shape the future of financial education while preparing students for success in an evolving financial services profession. The Program Chair provides leadership for all aspects of the Financial Planning and Financial Management programs, including oversight of curriculum quality, program assessment, accreditation and certification alignment, faculty development, and continuous improvement initiatives. This position serves as the subject matter expert for the programs, leads curriculum development and revision efforts, approves course design and development, oversees course scheduling and staffing, and ensures program alignment with applicable accreditation standards and CFP® Board educational requirements. The Program Chair is responsible for recruiting, mentoring, assigning, observing, and evaluating lead faculty and adjunct faculty while fostering a collaborative and student-centered teaching environment. Additional responsibilities include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses within the discipline, partnering with Enrollment Management, Student Affairs, Transfer Credit, and other University departments to support student success, leading annual program assessment activities, developing and maintaining a Program Advisory Board, collaborating with Marketing to support program growth, participating in University committees and governance, engaging in scholarship and professional development, and representing Franklin University through professional associations and community partnerships. The ideal candidate will possess an earned doctorate in Finance, Financial Planning, or a closely related discipline from an institutionally (formerly regionally) accredited college or university, with a minimum of 18 graduate semester credit hours in the discipline. Candidates must have at least five years of university-level teaching experience and five years of progressively responsible leadership or management experience, along with demonstrated success in curriculum development, faculty leadership, assessment, and student engagement. The successful candidate will possess exceptional communication, collaboration, organizational, and leadership skills, demonstrate a commitment to student success and academic excellence, and be able to effectively manage multiple priorities in a dynamic higher education environment. Professional credentials such as Certified Financial Planner™ (CFP®) and/or Chartered Financial Analyst® (CFA®) , along with significant professional experience in the financial services industry, are strongly preferred. About Franklin Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults who must balance their educational goals with other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin has earned its long-standing reputation as an innovator in online education. The University offers applied, in-demand undergraduate, master's and doctorate programs that enable adult learners to achieve their educational and professional goals. From entrepreneurs to CEOs, more than 50,000 Franklin alumni can be found around the world contributing to the communities in which they live and work. Franklin offers a competitive benefits package, incentive program and free parking. In addition, Franklin provides free tuition assistance for employees and immediate family members. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status. We embrace diversity and strive to create an environment where students, faculty and staff feel a sense of belonging. We recognize that our collective potential can only be realized when difference is valued, equal access to opportunity is sought, and inclusion is practiced. Equal Opportunity Employer"
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=17f0e4d6-ec96-49ba-a74b-a2319b0f67a7": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, Mathematics (Introduction to Quantitative Reasoning)",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty, Mathematics (Introduction to Quantitative Reasoning). Franklin University, a private, independent, accredited, not-for-profit University located in Columbus, Ohio invites applicants for its Adjunct Faculty, Introduction to Quantitative Reasoning position during our Spring 2027 term. The Adjunct Faculty role includes primary responsibility for providing quality instruction and maintaining a positive learning environment with an emphasis placed on teaching, supporting and evaluating students. Position Summary: The Adjunct Faculty role includes primary responsibility for providing quality instruction and maintaining a positive learning environment with an emphasis placed on teaching, supporting and evaluating students. Assigned courses may be for face to face or online instruction This is an introductory-level math literacy course that covers algebraic topics and statistical concepts. Topics include: Algebraic Operations on Numbers, Linear Equations and Inequalities, Dimensional Analysis, Linear and Quadratic Functions, Probability and Statistics Duties and Responsibilities: Teaching the Introduction to Quantitative Reasoning course according to the curriculum, content, and outcomes as specified by the Lead Faculty responsible for the course. Timely assessment and evaluation of student assignments, including exams, homework assignments, quizzes, projects, discussions, and other activities related to the course. Monitoring and advising students on course-related work, prompt correspondence via email, facilitation of course-related discussions, holding regular office hours, and other assigned responsibilities corresponding to the faculty teaching assignment. Participation in adjunct Math faculty meetings and associated department activities as identified by the Lead Faculty for the assigned course, and responding to the Lead Faculty’s requests related to course components. Minimum Requirements/Qualifications: Master’s degree in Mathematics, Statistics, or a related area. If the degree is in a related area, then at least 18 graduate credit hours must be in Mathematics or Statistics. At least 3 years of teaching experience in collegiate Mathematics and Statistics. Proficient in online learning management systems Skilled in video conferencing platforms · Experience with digital grading tools Familiarity with math-specific instructional technology Demonstrated ability to use online proctoring tools for remote exams, including reviewing proctoring recordings/reports, documenting incidents, and following academic integrity procedures Commitment to teaching Mathematics to non-traditional, working students Flexibility and integrity in instructional practice Ability to work in a fast-paced environment Preferred Skills and Characteristics: Ph.D. in Mathematics or Statistics At least 5 years of teaching experience in collegiate Mathematics in various formats such as online, hybrid, etc. Experience with math-specific courseware platforms Proficiency with one or more online learning management systems Demonstrated proficiency with graphing calculators and/or math instructional technology Strong written and verbal communication skills, including timely, student-centered responsiveness About Franklin University Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults who must balance their educational goals with other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin has earned its long-standing reputation as an innovator in online education. The University offers applied, in-demand undergraduate, master’s and doctorate programs that enable adult learners to achieve their educational and professional goals. From entrepreneurs to CEOs, more than 50,000 Franklin alumni can be found around the world contributing to the communities in which they live and work. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status. We embrace diversity and strive to create an environment where students, faculty and staff feel a sense of belonging. We recognize that our collective potential can only be realized when difference is valued, equal access to opportunity is sought, and inclusion is practiced."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=e853850e-1afd-4021-bd98-0f7e60e5464e": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty-College Algebra",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty-College Algebra. Franklin University, a private, independent, accredited, not-for-profit University located in Columbus, Ohio invites applicants for its Adjunct Faculty-College Algebra position during our Spring 2027 term. The Adjunct Faculty role includes primary responsibility for providing quality instruction and maintaining a positive learning environment with an emphasis placed on teaching, supporting and evaluating students. This course is designed to prepare students for Applied Calculus and Discrete Mathematics and to provide the mathematical background needed for the analytic reasoning used in other courses. Topics include functions and their graphs, including exponential and logarithmic functions; complex numbers; systems of equations and inequalities; matrices; basic principles of counting and probability; and other selected topics. Note, this course has proctored exam(s). This role will work an online schedule. Duties and Responsibilities: Teaches courses in discipline Maintains attendance records, determines and submits grades with established timeframes established by the University Creates a welcoming, inclusive and productive learning environment Supports the resolution of student issues related to course instruction Provides feedback on what is working and what is not with the course Participates in all department meetings and required training Participates in personal professional development Performs other duties as assigned by the Lead Faculty Minimum Requirements/Qualifications: Minimum of an earned Master's degree with 18 graduate credit hours in Mathematics, Statistics or a related area, from a regionally accredited college or university At least 3 years of teaching experience in collegiate Mathematics Previous online teaching experience Commitment to collaborative & collegiate relationships Maintain confidential and sensitive information Knowledge of higher education regulations, compliance and accrediting agencies Computer proficiency Strong organizational and interpersonal skills Ability to communicate in a professional manner and work effectively with faculty, staff, students and members of the professional community Excellent multi-tasking skills Ability to work in a fast-paced environment Preferred Skills and Characteristics: A PhD in Mathematics or Statistics About Franklin University Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults who must balance their educational goals with other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin has earned its long-standing reputation as an innovator in online education. The University offers applied, in-demand undergraduate, master’s and doctorate programs that enable adult learners to achieve their educational and professional goals. From entrepreneurs to CEOs, more than 50,000 Franklin alumni can be found around the world contributing to the communities in which they live and work. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status. We embrace diversity and strive to create an environment where students, faculty and staff feel a sense of belonging. We recognize that our collective potential can only be realized when difference is valued, equal access to opportunity is sought, and inclusion is practiced."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=40b3ee0f-522e-40cf-a59f-4e97049bc9bd": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, Computer Science",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty, Computer Science. Position Summary: Franklin University, a private, independent, accredited, not-for-profit University located in Columbus, Ohio invites applicants for its Adjunct Faculty-Computer Science positions The Adjunct Faculty role includes primary responsibility for providing quality instruction and maintaining a positive learning environment with an emphasis placed on teaching, supporting and evaluating students. Courses: COMP 620: Analysis of Algorithms COMP 691: Capstone for Master's in Computer Science AI 603: AI Programming Duties and Responsibilities: Teaches courses in discipline Maintains attendance records, determines and submits grades with established timeframes established by the University Creates a welcoming, inclusive and productive learning environment Supports the resolution of student issues related to course instruction Provides feedback on what is working and what is not with the course Participates in all department meetings and required training Participates in personal professional development Performs other duties as assigned by the Lead Faculty Minimum Requirements/Qualifications: A Doctoral degree in Computer Science, Computing Engineering, Software Engineering, AI, or a closely related area. Commitment to collaborative & collegiate relationships Maintain confidential and sensitive information Knowledge of higher education regulations, compliance and accrediting agencies Computer proficiency Strong organizational and interpersonal skills Ability to communicate in a professional manner and work effectively with faculty, staff, students and members of the professional community Excellent multi-tasking skills Ability to work in a fast-paced environment Preferred Skills and Characteristics: Previous teaching experience About Franklin University: Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults who must balance their educational goals with other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin has earned its long-standing reputation as an innovator in online education. The University offers applied, in-demand undergraduate, master’s and doctorate programs that enable adult learners to achieve their educational and professional goals. From entrepreneurs to CEOs, more than 50,000 Franklin alumni can be found around the world contributing to the communities in which they live and work."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=33e3a8ce-02da-491c-af2f-f416f5d50bdd": {
      "title": "Student Affairs Work Study Technician",
      "description": "Student Affairs Work Study Technician. Position Summary: The Student Affairs Operations Work Study Assistant will assist the department by providing administrative support for programs, services, and activities. The student may work with/for other staff in the unit on a project basis as time, skills, and need dictate. Duties and Responsibilities: • Filing • Phone outreach to students • Event coordination assistance • Process documentation assistance • Meeting minutes • Resume review support • Assist with data reports • Research requests • Special projects as assigned Minimum Requirements/Qualifications: • Must be a current Franklin student, and have Federal Work Study eligibility • Knowledge of Microsoft Office products • Strong organizational skills • Detail oriented • Dependable • Prompt • Excellent communication skills • Able to work independently and follow the direction of the supervisor • Comfortable working with highly confidential documents Preferred Skills and Characteristics: Ideal candidate must be able to work 20-25 hours per week, Monday-Friday, 8:00-5:00. Additional Notes: Ideal candidate must be able to work 20-25 hours per week, Monday-Friday, 8:00-5:00. About Franklin University Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults who must balance their educational goals with other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin has earned its long-standing reputation as an innovator in online education. The University offers applied, in-demand undergraduate, master’s and doctorate programs that enable adult learners to achieve their educational and professional goals. From entrepreneurs to CEOs, more than 50,000 Franklin alumni can be found around the world contributing to the communities in which they live and work.. At Franklin University, we embrace diversity and strive to create an environment where students, faculty and staff feel a sense of belonging. We recognize that our collective potential can only be realized when difference is valued, equal access to opportunity is sought, and inclusion is practiced. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=b8a11bdd-7f3d-4fa2-952c-167c4cac42b8": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty (HR Use Only)",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty (HR Use Only). Please note: This position is only intended for application by those already in discussion with our Franklin Faculty. If you were not specifically directed to apply here by Human Resources, your application will not be reviewed . Visit our Careers page for discipline-specific adjunct faculty employment opportunities: https://www.franklin.edu/about-us/careers-at-franklin. If there is not currently an opportunity available, please take a moment to create a Presence which saves your information for a future application. You will also have the option to create Job Alerts to receive emails when new opportunities are added that meet your search criteria. Position Summary: The Adjunct Faculty role includes primary responsibility for providing quality instruction and maintaining a positive learning environment with an emphasis placed on teaching, supporting and evaluating students. Assigned courses may be for face to face or online instruction. Duties and Responsibilities: Teaches courses in discipline Maintains attendance records, determines and submits grades with established timeframes established by the University Creates a welcoming, inclusive and productive learning environment Supports the resolution of student issues related to course instruction Provides feedback on what is working and what is not with the course Participates in all department meetings and required training Participates in personal professional development Performs other duties as assigned by the Lead Faculty Minimum Requirements/Qualifications: Minimum of an earned Master's degree with 18 graduate credit hours in field appropriate to the teaching discipline, from a regionally accredited college or university A Doctoral degree may be required to teach certain courses Commitment to collaborative & collegiate relationships Maintain confidential and sensitive information Knowledge of higher education regulations, compliance and accrediting agencies Computer proficiency Strong organizational and interpersonal skills Ability to communicate in a professional manner and work effectively with faculty, staff, students and members of the professional community Excellent multi-tasking skills Ability to work in a fast-paced environment Preferred Skills and Characteristics: Previous teaching experience About Franklin University Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults who must balance their educational goals with other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin has earned its long-standing reputation as an innovator in online education. The University offers applied, in-demand undergraduate, master’s and doctorate programs that enable adult learners to achieve their educational and professional goals. From entrepreneurs to CEOs, more than 50,000 Franklin alumni can be found around the world contributing to the communities in which they live and work. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=67a05a7d-4fd5-4966-a731-e1ac2353be3f": {
      "title": "Work Study",
      "description": "Work Study. Federal Work-Study students assist various departments within the university or community-based organizations. Work-study positions are intended to complement the student’s academic and career goals while fostering skill development in a professional setting. This position will serve as a support to staff and/or faculty at Franklin University. This person is considered to be a member of the team and is thus expected to demonstrate professional behavior at all times. ALL CANDIDATES MUST BE CURRENT FRANKLIN STUDENTS, AND HAVE FEDERAL WORK STUDY ELIGIBILITY. The Work Study is an integral part of the University and responsible for many of the day to day administrative duties to include but not limited to: Handle general administrative tasks, including filing, photocopying, scanning, and entering data. Conduct basic internet research to gather, analyze, and organize relevant information for projects, reports, and decision-making. Manage phone calls and correspondence, including emails and in-person inquiries. Organize and maintain office supplies and documents. Assist team members in their daily work load to support specific departmental needs. Assist team members in documentation including meeting notes Contribute to community service programs or outreach initiatives if placed in non-profit roles. All other duties/tasks as assigned. Candidates for a Federal Work-Study position should possess strong organizational and time-management abilities, along with effective communication and interpersonal skills. Basic computer literacy, including proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite and email platforms, is essential. Attention to detail and accuracy in completing tasks is critical, as is the ability to work both independently and collaboratively as part of a team. Professionalism and a commitment to maintaining confidentiality are also key qualifications for success in this role. The ideal candidate must be able to work 10-25 hours per week."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=abea1997-34a7-4073-a3f5-eabdb8683832": {
      "title": "Clinical Coordinator",
      "description": "Clinical Coordinator. Franklin University, a private, independent, accredited, nonprofit university located in Columbus, Ohio, invites applications for the position of Clinical Coordinator. Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults balancing educational goals with work, family, and other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin is recognized as an innovator in online education and serves students through applied undergraduate, master's, and doctoral programs designed to support career advancement and lifelong learning. More than 50,000 Franklin alumni live and work around the world, making meaningful contributions to their professions and communities. Position Summary: The Clinical Coordinator role includes primary responsibility for maintaining documentation of program clinical/internship requirements. The clinical coordinator will manage clinical site agreements, maintenance of documentation of student compliance with all school and agency requirements, preceptor documentation, and documentation for accreditation and regulatory needs. Duties and Responsibilities: Student contact for internship/clinical questions/issues. Site contact for internship/clinical questions/issues. Maintain, prepare, and collect required student and site internship/clinical forms. Liaison between College/Department and internship/clinical site for Affiliation Agreement completion. Build and maintain College/Department relations with appropriate internship/clinical sites. Coordinate and track student health screenings and background checks. Build and maintain student evaluation system Maintain multiple databases, including EXXAT, BI, Sharepoint, Excel Support identification of sites for student placement Obtain and verify site-specific requirements. Provide guidance to students on internship/clinical responsibilities, procedures, and conduct. Create and distribute reports on sites, students, and complete projects for assessment and accreditation. Maintain credential file on site supervisors. Collaborate with University, College, and Department to evaluate and improve internship/clinical policies and procedures. Maintain the clinical documentation system Register students in clinical, and clinical courses in Colleague and Self-Service Communication emails to students as they approach graduation, adding this information into colleague for registrar and advising reference Administrative duties as assigned Minimum Requirements/Qualifications: Bachelor's degree required Commitment to collaborative & collegiate relationships • Strong organizational and interpersonal skills Ability to communicate in a professional manner and work effectively with faculty, staff and members of the professional community Excellent multi-tasking skills Ability to work in a fast-paced environment Demonstrated strong communication skills, both oral and written, are required in order to customize communication pieces to effectively reach intended audience and prepare reports. The candidate must have strong computer skills, including the Microsoft Office suite of products, as well as other databases and a demonstrated background in internet-based research. Strong attention to detail, demonstrated active listening skills and process orientation Must be flexible, able to prioritize, multitask and manage several projects simultaneously in a time sensitive environment. Must be able to work both independently and collaboratively with multiple University departments and operate with a high degree of confidentiality. Preferred Skills and Characteristics: Knowledge of higher education regulations, compliance and accrediting agencies Experience with student placement Experience with clinical management systems Previous work experience in higher education in student services, academic affairs, or similar Healthcare background Experience in EXXAT and Excel Additional Notes: Required to be available for appointments, onsite meetings and other university activities Some travel may be required on a limited basis Occasional weekend events"
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=f876a7c0-4405-4c45-95ca-78f969c98328": {
      "title": "Director of Market Intelligence and Planning",
      "description": "Director of Market Intelligence and Planning. Franklin University, a private, independent, accredited, nonprofit university located in Columbus, Ohio, invites applications for the position of Director of Market Intelligence and Planning. Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults balancing educational goals with work, family, and other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin is recognized as an innovator in online education and serves students through applied undergraduate, master's, and doctoral programs designed to support career advancement and lifelong learning. More than 50,000 Franklin alumni live and work around the world, making meaningful contributions to their professions and communities. Position Summary The Director leads a cross-functional Marketing Insights Team, represents Marketing in strategic planning and implementation meetings, and facilitates the development of integrated marketing plans in partnership with Franklin’s CSGI (Center for Strategic Growth & Innovation), university stakeholders and marketing channel owners. Leading the Integrated Marketing area, the role is responsible for research and program marketing functions and oversees marketing initiatives related to new-program launches and high-growth programs. Key Responsibilities Represent Marketing in CSGI planning and facilitate integrated marketing plans for approved growth priorities in partnership with CSGI, university stakeholders and channel owners. Oversee the market research function and cross-functional Marketing Insights Team to identify program and market opportunities and secure leadership input on their potential. Operate market research tools and platforms to assess markets, analyze competitive conditions and uncover insights that support program and growth decisions. Translate approved opportunities into recommendations for business development and areas where the University should concentrate effort and resources. Oversee program marketing, including new-program launches, program-level market expertise and strategies to maximize high-growth programs. Prepare decision-ready recommendations regarding market priorities, program positioning, portfolio opportunities and launch readiness. Evaluate program, audience and market performance with channel owners and use the findings to refine future plans. Lead execution of high-level marketing plans and establish clear planning milestones and handoffs to Digital Strategy and Marketing Operations, and Brand Communications. Supervise the Program Marketing Manager, provide direction to the part-time Marketing Coordinator, and build alignment across CSGI leadership, academic, enrollment and business development teams. Required Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in marketing, business, communications, research or a related field. Seven years of progressively responsible experience in integrated marketing, market planning, program or product marketing, market research or marketing strategy. Experience working with business development teams and tools to inform decisions on market development and viability. At least three years of experience leading employees, major initiatives or cross-functional teams. Experience overseeing market research projects and managing external research partners. Demonstrated ability to apply research and performance data, translate complex information and produce clear recommendations. Strong planning, communication, presentation and relationship-management skills, with experience coordinating work across multiple departments. Preferred Qualifications Master of Business Administration. Experience with program, product or portfolio marketing and new-program or new-product launches. Higher education, enrollment marketing or adult-learner marketing experience. Experience working with business development, academic or institutional planning teams. Additional Information This position follows an on-site hybrid work schedule and requires flexibility to meet departmental and university needs. Evening and weekend events may be required, and some travel is expected. Franklin offers a competitive benefits package, incentive program, and free parking. In addition, Franklin provides free undergraduate tuition for employees and eligible family members, graduate tuition assistance for full-time employees and eligible family members, and a discount on doctoral tuition for employees. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status."
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/FRA1012FRANK/JobBoard/e51857cc-39da-44b8-a30e-839c982ff791/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=e92d164f-3d57-41b9-8c7f-735c334a4bf2": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, Surgical Technology",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty, Surgical Technology. Franklin University, a private, independent, accredited, not-for-profit University located in Columbus, Ohio invites applicants for its Surgical Technology Adjunct Role. The Adjunct Faculty role includes primary responsibility for providing quality instruction and maintaining a positive learning environment with an emphasis placed on teaching, supporting and evaluating students. Position Summary: The Adjunct Faculty role includes primary responsibility for providing quality instruction and maintaining a positive learning environment with an emphasis placed on teaching, supporting and evaluating students. Assigned courses will be for online instruction. Courses Available to Teach and Course Descriptions/Outcomes: HEA 1001: Professionalism in Allied Health: Discuss the expectations of an allied healthcare professional in the workplace. Use emotional intelligence, self-management, and interpersonal skills to communicate effectively in the workplace. Assess internal and external communication skills with all exchanges. Prioritize the patient care experience with successful interactions and patient satisfaction. Construct solution-oriented conversations, conflict management strategies, and productive action plans. HEA 1012: Introduction to Surgical Technician I: Demonstrate the skills and knowledge of the competent, entry-level Surgical Technologist. Describe the instrumentation and equipment used in the operating room. Identity examples of using a strong surgical conscience, demonstrating accountability, and understanding actions needed. Describe appropriate ethical, professional, and respectful values while providing patient care. HEA 1022: Introduction to Surgical Technician II: This course includes topics related to infection control, use of personal protective equipment, surgical pharmacology, sterile technique, decontamination and sterilization standards, the intraoperative and postoperative focus and surgical instrumentation and equipment. It also addresses surgical pharmacology, aspects of medical law related to the healthcare setting, support of the operative patient, and safety in the operating room. SURG 101: Foundations of Surgical Technology: This course sets the foundation for the role of a surgical technologist in today's healthcare environment. Focus will be on the areas of responsibilities, law and ethics, and developing a career plan to advance in this critical field. SURG 101C: Foundations of Surgical Technology Clinical: This course provides hands-on application of foundational surgical technology skills in a hospital facility, surgical center, or other appropriate site. Students will practice basic aseptic techniques, surgical instrumentation handling, and perioperative patient care. Simulated surgical procedures will reinforce theoretical knowledge and develop fundamental competencies necessary for clinical practice. SURG 102: Surgical Techniques: This course explores infection control, sterilization, and aseptic techniques essential to surgical practice. Students will learn to disrupt the chain of infection, classify wounds, and understand wound healing. Emphasis is placed on proper hand hygiene, PPE use, sterilization packaging methods, and the complex life cycle of surgical cases, including patient status, technique, instrumentation, and equipment, ensuring patient and provider safety. SURG 102C: Surgical Techniques Clinical: Building upon foundational skills, this course focuses on the application of surgical techniques in simulated and clinical environments. Students will refine their aseptic techniques, surgical case preparation, and intraoperative skills under faculty supervision. Clinical observation and participation in basic surgical procedures will introduce students to real-world surgical settings. SURG 104: Sterile Processing: This course provides comprehensive instruction in sterile processing, equipping students with the knowledge and skills necessary to ensure the proper decontamination, sterilization, storage, and distribution of surgical instruments and medical equipment. Topics include infection control, microbiology, instrument identification, sterilization techniques, inventory management, and regulatory compliance. Emphasis is placed on industry standards, patient safety, and best practices in central sterile services. Upon completion, students will be prepared to sit for the Certified Registered Central Service Technician (CRCST) certification exam SURG 201: Surgical Procedures: This course is the first of two that will build the student's practical skills for assisting in the operating room using various instrumentation, safety protocols, and procedures for general and specialized surgeries. Students will develop their hands-on acumen through practical demonstrations of each skill acquired. SURG 201C: Surgical Procedures Clinical: This course enhances students’ clinical readiness by increasing their participation in surgical procedures. Emphasis is placed on advanced intraoperative techniques, assisting in specialty surgeries, and applying problem-solving skills in dynamic surgical environments. Students will complete assigned clinical cases to meet accreditation requirements. SURG 202: Complex Surgical Procedures: This course is the second of two that will build the student's practical skills for assisting in the operating room using various instrumentation, safety protocols, and procedures for general and specialized surgeries. Students will develop their hands-on acumen through practical demonstrations of each skill acquired. SURG 202C Complex Surgical Procedures Clinical: As the culminating experiential course, students will refine their surgical technology competencies through extensive clinical practice. This capstone experience focuses on independent surgical case management, professional readiness, and mastery of accreditation-required competencies. Students will prepare for transition into professional surgical technologist roles. Duties and Responsibilities: Teaches courses in discipline Maintains attendance records, determines and submits grades with established timeframes established by the University Creates a welcoming, inclusive and productive learning environment Supports the resolution of student issues related to course instruction Provides feedback on what is working and what is not with the course Participates in all department meetings and required training Participates in personal professional development Performs other duties as assigned by the Lead Faculty Minimum Requirements/Qualifications: Bachelor's Degree Certified Surgical Technologist (CST) credential Commitment to collaborative & collegiate relationships Maintain confidential and sensitive information Knowledge of higher education regulations, compliance and accrediting agencies Strong organizational and interpersonal skills Ability to communicate in a professional manner and work effectively with faculty, staff, students and members of the professional community Excellent multi-tasking skills Ability to work in a fast-paced environment Preferred Skills and Characteristics: Previous teaching experience Master Degree We are not able to hire in California, New York, Massachusetts, Montana, or the City of Philadelphia at this time. About Franklin University Founded in 1902, Franklin University has built its reputation on removing barriers to higher education for ambitious adults who must balance their educational goals with other commitments. Accredited and nonprofit, Franklin has earned its long-standing reputation as an innovator in online education. The University offers applied, in-demand undergraduate, master’s and doctorate programs that enable adult learners to achieve their educational and professional goals. From entrepreneurs to CEOs, more than 50,000 Franklin alumni can be found around the world contributing to the communities in which they live and work. Franklin University is committed to creating a respectful and inclusive educational and workplace environment. Franklin does not discriminate on the basis of age, religion, race, ethnicity, color, national origin, ancestry, immigration status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, marital or familial status, disability, or veteran or military status. We embrace diversity and strive to create an environment where students, faculty and staff feel a sense of belonging. We recognize that our collective potential can only be realized when difference is valued, equal access to opportunity is sought, and inclusion is practiced."
    }
  },
